# Fasha

Fasha is a web platform for finding volunteering opportunities in Rwanda, proposing your own
community initiatives, and building a record of service that someone else can verify.

I built it for my capstone project, which looks at why volunteering in Rwanda is harder to
access, join and report than it needs to be. The research found three problems, and the platform
is organised around them.

The first is discovery. Opportunities are shared across social media pages, word of mouth and
individual NGO websites, so there is no single place to look. If you are not already inside
someone's network, you tend not to hear about anything. Fasha puts everything in one feed and
ranks it by how well it fits the skills and interests you have listed.

The second is who gets to start things. Umuganda and most organised volunteering are planned from
the top down, which leaves people with few structured ways to act on needs they have noticed
themselves. On Fasha any volunteer can propose an initiative. It is reviewed before it goes
public, but the idea can come from anyone.

The third is proof. Attendance is usually recorded on paper registers that are slow to consolidate
and easy to lose, so volunteers end up with no reliable record of what they have done. Fasha
records check in and check out against a code that changes every 30 seconds, lets organisers
review virtual work, and credits approved hours to a portfolio page the volunteer can share with
an employer or a scholarship panel.

**Live app:** https://fasha-frontend.vercel.app
**API:** https://fashas-backend-eight.vercel.app
**Repository:** https://github.com/lilalajemima/fasha
**Walkthrough video:**:https://drive.google.com/drive/u/0/folders/1gsJXpxpPxZ-ZbGQMlDezs7ed79X_vXbH 
