**Author:** Lilala Runiga Jemima, BSc Software Engineering, African Leadership University

The platform is now complete and deployed. The frontend, the API and the database are all live,
and the mock data layer the earlier sprint ran on has been removed. What is left before the pilot
is the field testing itself.

---

## What it does

### For a volunteer

You sign up, fill in the skills and interests you have from a fixed list, and say which parts of
the week you are usually free. Discover then shows you everything published in the district with
a match score against your profile, and you can filter by format, sector, skill or how soon
applications close. You apply with a short statement.

If the work happens somewhere, you turn up and check in with the code the organiser is showing,
then check out when you leave. If it is remote, you send in what you produced and the organiser
reviews it. Either way the hours land on your portfolio once somebody else has confirmed them.

You can also propose your own initiative. It goes to the community admin for approval, and once
published you run it yourself, with the same tools an organisation gets, from inside the
initiative page.

### For an organisation

You post opportunities, in person or remote, and can attach a PDF brief if there is more to say.
Applicants arrive with a match score and the specific skills and interests you have in common, so
the number means something rather than just ranking people. You accept or reject.

On the day you open check in, and the screen shows a QR code and a six digit code that both
change every thirty seconds. The register fills as people arrive, and you can mark somebody
present by hand if their phone will not cooperate. Afterwards you close the register and the
hours are credited. For remote work you review what was sent in, and rejecting it requires a
reason, because hours refused with nothing said are the fastest way to make somebody stop
volunteering.

### For the community admin

The overview carries a calendar of everything in the district for the last twelve months, with
each entry marked for whether it is virtual or in person and whether an organisation or a
resident is running it. The approvals queue holds initiatives proposed by residents. The activity
page exports events and attendance as CSV. The user table is where accounts are deactivated,
reinstated, given a temporary password or removed.

---

## Tools used

| Layer | Choice | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Frontend | React 19, TypeScript, Vite | Volunteers test this on their own phones, so it had to be a browser app with no install step |
| Routing | React Router 7 | Nested layout routes let all three workspaces share one shell |
| Styling | Plain CSS with custom properties | No framework weight, and one file maps the Figma design to the code |
| Backend | Node.js, Express 5, TypeScript | A single modular monolith, as set out in the proposal |
| ORM | Prisma 7 | Typed queries, and migrations I can version and replay |
| Database | PostgreSQL via Supabase | Native array columns, which is how matching compares skill and interest tags |
| Auth | bcrypt, JSON Web Tokens | Hashed passwords and stateless protected routes |
| Verification | Node crypto HMAC, qrcode | The event code is a one time password derived from the clock, so a photographed code expires |
| Unit tests | Vitest | Fast enough to run on every save, on both halves |
| End to end tests | Playwright | Drives three real browser engines and two phone profiles |
| Containers | Docker Compose | So the whole thing runs from one command on a machine that is not mine |
| Fonts | Fugaz One, Plus Jakarta Sans | Display and body faces from the design system |

---

## Project structure

```
fasha/
├── docker-compose.yml          runs both halves together
├── docs/testing/               load test and Lighthouse results, kept as evidence
├── frontend/
│   ├── e2e/                    Playwright specs
│   ├── scripts/                the Lighthouse runner and its summariser
│   ├── nginx.conf              serves the built app in the container
│   ├── playwright.config.ts    five browser projects
│   ├── vitest.config.ts
│   └── src/
│       ├── styles/
│       │   ├── tokens.css      every colour, space and radius, taken from Figma
│       │   └── workspace.css   tables, toolbars, banners, cards, the calendar grid
│       ├── components/         Button, Card, Field, pickers, the QR scanner
│       ├── auth/               session state and the four route guards
│       ├── layouts/            one shell, and the navigation table all three roles read
│       ├── lib/                the logic that is not React, and its unit tests
│       ├── notifications/      the unread count in the top bar
│       └── pages/
│           ├── auth/           SignIn, SignUp, Forgotten
│           ├── volunteer/      Discover, MyActivity, MyInitiatives, Portfolio, CheckIn
│           ├── manage/         Overview, Opportunities, Applicants, Attendance, Deliverables
│           ├── admin/          Overview, Approvals, Activity, Users
│           ├── Profile.tsx     shared by all three roles
│           └── Privacy.tsx     public, reachable without an account
└── backend/
    ├── api/index.ts            the Vercel entry point
    ├── scripts/                the smoke test and the load test
    ├── prisma/
    │   ├── schema.prisma       the data model: 10 models and 8 enums
    │   └── migrations/         applied in order, never by hand
    └── src/
        ├── core/               things that are not about any one feature
        ├── middleware/         authenticate and authorise
        ├── modules/            one folder per feature, six files each
        └── app.ts
```

Each half has its own README with the detail:

- [backend/README.md](backend/README.md) — the module pattern, the API, the schema, how to add a feature
- [frontend/README.md](frontend/README.md) — the shell, the guards, the data hook, the test suites

In the earlier sprint every screen read its data through `frontend/src/mocks/api.ts`. That was
deliberate: each function in that file became a `fetch` call to the API, and no screen component
had to change when it did. The mock layer has since been deleted.

---

## Setting up the environment and the project

### What you need first

- Node.js 20 and npm. Check with `node -v`.
- Git.
- A PostgreSQL database. A free Supabase project is quickest, but a local Postgres 16 install
  works just as well.
- Docker Desktop, only if you want to run it in containers.

### Clone it

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lilalajemima/fasha.git
cd fasha
```

### Backend

```bash
cd backend
npm install
```

Create `backend/.env`:

```
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres"
JWT_SECRET="a long random string"
JWT_EXPIRES_IN="7d"
PORT=4000
CLIENT_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173"
```

There are two database URLs because Prisma needs a direct connection to run migrations, while the
running server uses the pooled one. `prisma.config.ts` reads `DIRECT_URL` for that reason.

Then set up the database and start it:

```bash
npm run db:migrate
npm run db:seed      # optional, makes a volunteer, an organisation and an admin
npm run dev          # http://localhost:4000
```

`npm run db:seed` needs the `SEED_*` variables in `.env` as well: a name and an email for each of
the three roles, and a password of at least eight characters.

`.env` is in `.gitignore` and should stay out of the repository.

### Frontend

In a second terminal:

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev          # http://localhost:5173
```

It expects the API at `http://localhost:4000`. To point it elsewhere, set `VITE_API_URL` in
`frontend/.env`.

Setting `VITE_DEMO_VOLUNTEER_EMAIL`, `VITE_DEMO_ORGANISATION_EMAIL` and `VITE_DEMO_PASSWORD` makes
a demo panel appear on the sign in page, so somebody can look around without making an account.
Leave them unset and the panel does not exist.

The other commands:

```bash
npm run build     # type checks and builds into dist/
npm run lint      # ESLint
npm run preview   # serves the built bundle
```

Run `npm run build` before you commit. The dev server is forgiving about things like an unused
import, and the build is not, which is useful.

---

## Running it with Docker

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

The API is on 4000 and the web app on 8081. The API container applies migrations before it
starts, so `backend/.env` has to exist and `DATABASE_URL` has to be reachable from inside the
container.

```bash
curl http://localhost:4000/health     # should return {"status":"ok"}
docker compose down                    # when you are finished
```

Both services have `restart: unless-stopped`, so if you leave them running they come back the
next time Docker starts and take port 4000 with them, which stops `npm run dev` working.

If you move either port, move both. The browser is told where the API is when the frontend image
is built, and the API only accepts the origin named in `CLIENT_ORIGIN`. Change one and the page
loads but every request fails.

---

## Testing

| What | Command | Covers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Backend units | `npm --prefix backend test` | 169 tests over the services, where the rules are |
| Frontend units | `npm --prefix frontend test` | 58 tests over the pure functions in `lib` |
| End to end | `npm --prefix frontend run e2e` | 102 tests across three browser engines and two phones |
| API smoke | `npm --prefix backend run smoke` | A whole scenario against a running API |
| Load | `npm --prefix backend run load` | Three endpoints at 10, 25 and 50 concurrent users |
| Lighthouse | `npm --prefix frontend run lighthouse` | Performance and accessibility on the public pages |

Both unit suites use in-memory fakes and pure functions, so they need nothing running and finish
in about a second each.

The end to end suite needs the app running. It drives real browsers against the real API, so it
creates real accounts and events. Do not point it at production. The journey test runs two
browser contexts at once, because the code on the organiser's screen is what the volunteer has to
type, which is not something one session can fake.

The load test measures three endpoints chosen so the gaps mean something: `/catalogue` touches no
database, `/health` is one `SELECT 1`, and `/opportunities` is a real query. The difference
between the first two is what reaching the database costs, and the difference between the second
and third is what Fasha costs.

Results kept as evidence are in [docs/testing](docs/testing).

---

## Deployment

### Where it runs

| Part | Platform | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Web client | Vercel | Static build from `frontend/`, served from a CDN |
| API | Vercel | Serverless function built from `backend/`, pinned to the `fra1` region |
| Database | Supabase | Managed PostgreSQL in `eu-central-1`, pooled for the app and direct for migrations |
| Files | Postgres | Pictures and briefs are stored as base64 on the row, not in object storage |

I went with managed services rather than hosting anything myself. The pilot runs for a month with
25 to 40 users, which the free tiers cover comfortably, and neither I nor the people testing it
should depend on a machine that has to stay switched on.

The API and the database are in the same region deliberately. Load testing from my own machine in
Kigali showed around 185ms for a single database round trip, almost all of it distance. In
production the two sit in the same city, so that cost very nearly disappears.

### Releasing

1. Merge into `main` after review.
2. Vercel rebuilds both projects automatically. There is nothing to run by hand.
3. The API container applies `prisma migrate deploy` on start. Migrations live in
   `backend/prisma/migrations/` and are applied in order, never by hand.
4. Check it: `GET /health` returns 200, then sign in, load the feed and do one check in.
5. If something is wrong, roll back to the previous deployment in the Vercel dashboard.
   Migrations are written to be additive, so rolling back does not lose data.

### Configuration

No secret is committed. Vercel holds `DATABASE_URL`, `DIRECT_URL`, `JWT_SECRET` and
`CLIENT_ORIGIN` for the API, and `VITE_API_URL` plus the three demo variables for the client.

---

## What changed from the plan

A few things are different from the original proposal, and they were all decisions rather than
omissions.

**The API moved from Render to Vercel.** Keeping both halves on one platform meant one dashboard,
one set of environment variables and one deployment to check. The cost is cold starts: the first
request after a quiet period takes about two seconds while the function wakes up, which a pilot
used mostly around Umuganda will feel.

**Files are stored in the database rather than in Supabase Storage.** Profile pictures are resized
to 256px in the browser and stored as base64, and briefs are capped at a megabyte. It avoids a
second set of credentials and a bucket to manage. At a few hundred accounts it should move.

**Skills and interests are chosen from a fixed list rather than typed.** Matching compares tags
word for word, and when either side could invent its own, "Graphic design", "graphics" and
"designing posters" were three different tags and the score meant nothing.

**There is no email.** A forgotten password is handled by a community admin issuing a temporary
one, which the person replaces at their next sign in. That works in a sector office where people
can walk in, and does not scale beyond it.

---

## Thinking about the pilot

People will use this on their own phones, on mobile data, outdoors. That shaped a few decisions:
it is a browser app so there is nothing to install, the production bundle is kept small, the
colours are high contrast rather than pale, and the QR check in has a manual fallback for the
moments when the network drops.

It also shaped the testing. The end to end suite runs on a Pixel and an iPhone profile as well as
on desktop, and Lighthouse measures the public pages on a simulated mid-range phone over
throttled 4G rather than on a laptop, because that is closer to the person the platform is for.

---

## Common issues

**Issue: the frontend loads but every request fails**

- `CLIENT_ORIGIN` on the API has to match exactly where the page is served from:
  `http://localhost:5173` for the dev server, `http://localhost:8081` for Docker, the Vercel URL
  in production
- the browser cannot tell JavaScript the difference between a rejected origin and a dead server,
  so the app reports both as "cannot reach the server"
- check with `curl -i -H "Origin: http://localhost:5173" http://localhost:4000/health`

**Issue: `npm run dev` on the backend exits straight away**

- a Docker container is probably still holding port 4000
- `docker compose down`, then try again
- `netstat -ano | grep :4000` shows what has it

**Issue: TypeScript says a field does not exist on a Prisma model that is in the schema**

- the generated client is behind: `npm --prefix backend run db:generate`
- if that does not help, delete `backend/node_modules/.prisma`. It is a leftover from an older
  Prisma version and gets picked up first
- `prisma migrate dev` skips generating when there is nothing to apply, so a migration that ran
  earlier can leave the client stale with no sign anything is wrong

**Issue: changes are not showing on the live site**

- Vercel builds what is on GitHub, not what is on your machine
- `git status -sb` says whether you have commits you have not pushed